<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <!--
    The shape half of the design system (docs/design/handoff.md §"Geometry").

    Everything sits on a 4px grid. These are theme-independent: a corner radius does not change
    when the OS flips to light, so they live outside `ThemeDictionaries` and resolve in either
    variant.
  -->

  <!-- Radii. Four steps plus the pill. Panels and cards are the roundest thing in the app;
       keycaps and the little kbd chips are tighter, so a dense board does not read as blobby. -->
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusPanel">8</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusControl">5</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusKeycap">4</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusChip">3</CornerRadius>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusPill">999</CornerRadius>

  <!-- Padding rhythms. Avalonia's two-value Thickness is "horizontal,vertical", which is the
       reverse of the CSS shorthand the handoff is written in: its "8,13" button padding is
       13 horizontal by 8 vertical, hence "13,8" here. -->
  <Thickness x:Key="PaddingCard">14</Thickness>
  <Thickness x:Key="PaddingInspectorSection">12</Thickness>
  <Thickness x:Key="PaddingButton">13,8</Thickness>
  <Thickness x:Key="PaddingTab">13,0</Thickness>

  <!-- The spacing scale. Nothing in a view should use a gap that is not one of these six. -->
  <x:Double x:Key="Space4">4</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="Space8">8</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="Space12">12</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="Space16">16</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="Space24">24</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="Space32">32</x:Double>

  <!-- Chrome. Fixed heights, because the toolbar and the tab strip must not grow with their
       content — a device with a long name may not push the board down. -->
  <x:Double x:Key="HeightToolbar">46</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="HeightTabBar">38</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="HeightAdvisoryStrip">30</x:Double>

  <!-- Rails and gutters. The inspector widens only on the macro-editing variant, where the
       recorded step list needs the room.

       Since issue #119 the rail is DRAG-ADJUSTABLE, so 268 is the width it starts at rather than
       the width it keeps, and the wide value is a FLOOR the Macro panel raises it to rather than a
       value that replaces the user's. Min and Max bound the drag: 240 is the narrowest the header's
       tile, token and assignment line still fit on one line; 520 is where the rail stops being a
       rail and starts being half the window on the 1000px default.

       THE FLOOR IS 480 SINCE ISSUE #148, AND THAT IS A DEVIATION FROM docs/design/handoff.md:90-96,
       which says 300 on the macro-editing variant. The redesigned Macro panel puts a step row's
       grip, key chip, action word, delay pill and delete mark on ONE line, and the composer under
       it draws four modifier chips, a key field and `Record key` on another; at 300 both wrapped,
       and a wrapped step row is exactly what the fixed-height list exists to make scannable. #146
       took it to 440 on those two rows.

       480 IS A MEASURED NUMBER, NOT AN ESTIMATE. #148 put the compose bar's action and delay
       controls on ONE line, as the mock draws them — `tap` `press` `release` · then wait · `fixed`
       `random` · [ 80 ] ms. Captured at the floor with the widest state of every control (the
       selected segment is SemiBold, which is a pixel wider than the resting one), that row measures
       432 px and the panel's own chrome takes 50 more, so 482 is the smallest width it fits in and
       480 is what it fits in once the millisecond field is allowed to give way to its 48 px
       MinWidth. Two levers came first and both were spent: the `none` segment went (60 px) and the
       segments came down to the segmented family's own 11,4 / 11px (`ComposerSegment`, 35 px). It
       is still a FLOOR, so a user who has dragged wider keeps their width — and Max moved with it,
       because a floor the seam cannot be dragged past by a useful margin is an override wearing a
       floor's name. -->
  <x:Double x:Key="WidthInspectorRail">268</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="WidthInspectorRailWide">480</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="WidthInspectorRailMin">240</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="WidthInspectorRailMax">560</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="GutterSplit">26</x:Double>

  <!-- The dashboard card grid: two columns, and a 2px status rail flush against the left edge
       of every card.

       The gap reaches the grid as the two Thicknesses beside it, because Avalonia's UniformGrid
       has no spacing property and a Margin is a Thickness that no markup can compose out of an
       x:Double. Each cell carries the gap on its right and bottom; the panel carries the negative
       of it, which inflates its slot by exactly one gap in each direction so the last column ends
       on the viewport's edge and the scroll extent stops at the bottom of the last row. The three
       are one number and are pinned to each other by ShapeAndTypeTokenTests - change the gap and
       the siblings have to follow, or the suite says so. -->
  <x:Double x:Key="CardGridGap">12</x:Double>
  <Thickness x:Key="CardGridCellGap">0,0,12,12</Thickness>
  <Thickness x:Key="CardGridPanelBleed">0,0,-12,-12</Thickness>
  <x:Double x:Key="WidthCardStatusRail">2</x:Double>

  <!-- Every dashboard card is exactly this tall, whichever state it is in: the card keeps its size
       and its buttons keep their positions, so only the status line and a 3px indeterminate bar
       change between states and a scan can never shift the layout under the cursor (mockup 2e).

       Derived from the mockup canvas' own box model for the tallest state, the unwritable card
       with its two-line explanation: 2 border + 28 padding + 70 art frame (56 + 6 padding + 1
       border, doubled) + 12 + 51.9 explanation (2 x 15.95 + 18 padding + 2 border) + 12 + 30
       buttons = 205.9. Avalonia's line boxes are the font's own metrics rather than the mock's
       explicit line-heights, which puts the same anatomy at ~211 here (16.5 prose leading, a
       33.6 button). 212 is the next step of the 4px grid that clears both. -->
  <x:Double x:Key="HeightDeviceCard">212</x:Double>

  <!-- The keyboard canvas.

       The board is drawn on a cell pitch, not on a cap size: every 1U cell is KeycapPitchX wide
       and KeycapPitchY tall, and the cap inside it is inset by KeycapGap. Pitch minus gap is
       therefore the cap the handoff specifies - "keycaps at mock scale: 30x26 (1u), gap 4":

           34 - 4 = 30 wide      30 - 4 = 26 tall

       That subtraction is the load-bearing arithmetic of the whole canvas and is asserted in
       ShapeAndTypeTokenTests, so the three numbers cannot drift apart in silence. A wide key is
       still its span in pitches minus one gap (a 2U Backspace is 2 x 34 - 4 = 64), which is why
       the gap is a single inset and not a margin on each side.

       The two halves of the split board are two panels: each is padded by PaddingBoardSection,
       carries RadiusBoardSection, and the two are separated by the existing GutterSplit (26). -->
  <x:Double x:Key="KeycapPitchX">34</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="KeycapPitchY">30</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="KeycapGap">4</x:Double>
  <Thickness x:Key="PaddingBoardSection">9</Thickness>
  <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusBoardSection">7</CornerRadius>

  <!-- How far the picture may be grown (issue #123). The handoff caps nothing - "scale the whole
       board up with available space" - which was written against its own 1000px canvas and reads
       as absurd on a large display: at 2560 wide the Freestyle Edge RGB's 695x196 board was drawn
       2238x631, with 40px legends on caps the size of a fist.

       1.5 was that judgement's answer and it was too tight (issue #135): on a large display the
       board stopped growing with most of the window still empty, and users asked for the room. The
       cap is 3.0 - twice what #123 set - which is reached at ~2100px of board width, i.e. only on a
       genuinely large window. Below it nothing moved: the cap is not a size, it is where growth
       stops, and the 1000px default window and the 720x480 floor both sit well under it.

       IT IS STILL A CAP RATHER THAN "no limit". The 2560-wide case above is what it exists for -
       past some point a bigger picture stops being easier to read - and 3.0 moves that point out
       rather than removing it. Beyond it the board stays centred and the extra space becomes canvas.

       BoardScaleHost itself defaults to no cap: the policy is this token plus the one call site in
       Controls/KeyboardView.axaml, so a host used anywhere else is unaffected. -->
  <x:Double x:Key="BoardScaleMax">3.0</x:Double>

  <!-- And how far it may be shrunk. Fitting the board to its slot (issue #123) makes every row that
       shares the column a claim on the picture, and on the Lighting tab those rows can win: at the
       720x480 floor the paint line, the wrapped zone buttons and the header want 231 of the 235px
       the column has, which left the board FOUR PIXELS TALL. This is the floor its row keeps.

       100 rather than a round guess: at the 356px the Lighting board's column is wide at that
       window, the Freestyle Edge RGB fits at 0.511 by width and 0.510 by height, so the floor is
       the point where the two constraints meet rather than a number that overrides one of them. It
       is a floor on the ROW, not on the scale, so a taller board is simply drawn smaller.

       The Layout tab's board needs none: the only thing sharing its column is the 34px legend row,
       so nothing there can starve it. -->
  <x:Double x:Key="HeightBoardMin">100</x:Double>

  <!-- The keycap badge vocabulary (handoff.md, "Focus, selection, key badges"). One badge, one
       hue, one shape: a bar across the bottom edge for a remap, a dot top-right for a macro, a
       right-triangle bottom-right for tap-and-hold, a rounded bar top-right for an advisory.
       BadgeGap is what keeps two badges that share a corner apart. The colours are already
       tokens (BadgeMacroBrush, BadgeTapHoldBrush, StatusAdvisoryStrongBrush, AccentBrush). -->
  <x:Double x:Key="BadgeRemapBarHeight">2</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="BadgeMacroDotSize">5</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="BadgeTapHoldSize">6</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="BadgeAdvisoryBarWidth">12</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="BadgeAdvisoryBarHeight">3</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="BadgeGap">2</x:Double>

  <!-- Icons are geometry, never raster: a 16px box drawn with a 1.5px square-capped stroke. -->
  <x:Double x:Key="IconSize">16</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="IconStrokeThickness">1.5</x:Double>

  <!-- A dialog's type mark is drawn larger than a rail icon: the handoff's message box carries a
       24px mark, because it is the one thing that says what kind of interruption this is. -->
  <x:Double x:Key="IconSizeDialog">24</x:Double>

  <!-- The busy spinner: a 14px circle with one quadrant left transparent. -->
  <x:Double x:Key="SpinnerSize">14</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="SpinnerStrokeThickness">1.5</x:Double>

  <!-- Hatching, for a locked key and for an LED that is off: 45 degrees at a 4px pitch. -->
  <x:Double x:Key="HatchPitch">4</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="HatchAngle">45</x:Double>
</ResourceDictionary>
